---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ArticleHeader from "../components/ArticleHeader.astro";
import TableOfContents from "../components/TableOfContents.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { AUTHORS, DEFAULT_AUTHOR, authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import { SITE_NAME, SITE_URL } from "../data/site";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "How Much Protein Do I Need?",
    "url": "/how-much-protein-do-i-need/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Does Protein Powder Expire?",
    "url": "/does-protein-powder-expire/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Is Protein Powder Safe?",
    "url": "/is-protein-powder-safe/"
  }
];
const author = AUTHORS[DEFAULT_AUTHOR];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-18", modified: "2026-11-18" });
const title = "Homemade Protein Powder: 4 Recipes and the Honest Math";
const description =
  "Homemade protein powder tops out near 35-55% protein, vs about 90% for whey isolate. Four USDA-based recipes, grams per 25 g of protein, cost and safety.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Homemade Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/homemade-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you make homemade protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Weigh high-protein dry ingredients such as nonfat dry milk, defatted peanut flour, hemp hearts or pasteurized egg white powder, pulse any seeds briefly in a blender, whisk everything together and store it in an airtight jar. A simple mix of 300 g dry milk, 100 g peanut flour and 30 g cocoa gives about 25 g of protein per 66 g serving, per USDA values."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the highest-protein ingredient for homemade protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Dried egg white is the highest at 81.1 g of protein per 100 g in USDA FoodData Central, followed by defatted peanut flour at 52.2 g and defatted soy flour at 51.5 g. Nonfat dry milk carries about 35 g. Whole seeds such as hemp and pumpkin sit near 30 g but bring about half their weight as fat."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you make whey protein powder at home?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Liquid whey from straining yogurt or making cheese is mostly water and lactose, and even fully dried sweet whey is only 12.9 percent protein per USDA. Commercial whey concentrate and isolate reach 80 to 90 percent through industrial membrane filtration and spray-drying. Nonfat dry milk is the closest kitchen substitute, with milk protein at about 35 percent."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is homemade protein powder as good as store-bought?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is less concentrated. A homemade blend usually lands between 20 and 55 percent protein, so you need 45 to 120 g of powder and more calories to get 25 g of protein, compared with about 30 g of a whey isolate. Milk and egg based blends offer complete protein, while seed blends benefit from adding a legume such as peanut."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is homemade protein powder cheaper?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sometimes. Nonfat dry milk worked out to about $0.67 per 25 g of protein at the Amazon price we saw, well below premium whey at about $1.84. Hemp hearts at about $1.42 and PB2 at about $1.19 per 25 g are no cheaper than budget whey. Nuts and seeds bought in small packs usually cost more than finished powder."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does homemade protein powder last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the fat content. A low-fat blend of dry milk, peanut flour and cocoa keeps like other dry pantry goods when sealed and stored cool, and should be used by the earliest best-by date of its ingredients. Blends with ground seeds or nuts go rancid much faster, so make small batches, refrigerate or freeze them and finish them within a few weeks."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use protein powder from hemp seeds I grind myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, but ground hulled hemp seeds are only 31.6 percent protein and about 49 percent fat per USDA, so 25 g of protein costs about 438 calories. Commercial hemp protein powder is made from the pressed cake left after oil extraction, which is why it is far more concentrated. Pulse hemp with a dry flour so it does not turn into seed butter."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Protein Basics",
    tags: [
      "homemade protein powder",
      "homemade protein powder recipe",
      "how to make homemade protein powder",
      "diy protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Protein Powder Picks",
    link: "/best-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"How-To"} badge="Research-Backed" author={author} dates={dates} label="Our standards">
      <Fragment slot="title">Homemade Protein Powder</Fragment>
      What you can actually blend at home, how much protein each ingredient delivers per USDA data, and where a homemade mix stops making sense.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#ingredients">Best Ingredients</a>
      <a href="#recipes">Four Recipes</a>
      <a href="#why-not-whey">Why It Can't Match Whey</a>
      <a href="#cost">Is It Cheaper?</a>
      <a href="#safety">Food Safety</a>
      <a href="#allergens">Allergens and Health</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Homemade protein powder works, but it tops out at roughly 35 to 55 percent protein, while a whey isolate is about 90 percent. The gap comes from the fat, starch and lactose that whole foods carry and factories strip out. This guide uses USDA FoodData Central values to show what each kitchen ingredient delivers, four recipes with the math, real cost per 25 g, and the food-safety rules.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Homemade Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            You can make a useful protein powder at home from nonfat dry milk, powdered peanut butter, hemp hearts, pumpkin seeds and store-bought dried egg white, but expect about 25 g of protein per 45 to 70 g serving instead of per 30 g scoop. A dairy-and-peanut blend lands near 38 percent protein, a seed blend near 37 percent, and only a mix built around purchased egg white powder passes 50 percent, per our calculations from <a href="https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/" rel="noopener">USDA FoodData Central</a> values.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">38%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein in a dry milk + peanut flour blend</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">55%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Best case, with store-bought egg white</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~90%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical whey protein isolate</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$0.67</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Nonfat dry milk per 25 g protein (Amazon price seen)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The honest verdict: homemade is worth it if you want a cheap, whole-food shake booster and do not mind extra calories. If you want 25 g of protein in a small, low-calorie scoop, a tub from our <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> guide does a job no kitchen blender can.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Homemade blend</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein by weight</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Serving for 25 g protein</th>
