                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dry milk + peanut flour + cocoa</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">38%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">66 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 225</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hemp + pumpkin seed + peanut flour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">37%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">67 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 330</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Egg white powder + dry milk + cocoa</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">55%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">45 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 162</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oats + almonds + dry milk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">121 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 512</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Can You Use to Make Homemade Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best homemade protein powder ingredients are store-bought dried egg white at 81.1 g of protein per 100 g, defatted peanut flour at 52.2 g, nonfat dry milk at about 35 g, and hulled hemp seeds at 31.6 g, per USDA FoodData Central's SR Legacy records. Everything else people suggest, from oats to almonds to chia, sits below 22 percent protein and mostly adds calories.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ingredient (USDA FDC entry)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per 100 g</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories per 100 g</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Grams for 25 g protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories for 25 g protein</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Egg white, dried (172204)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">81.1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">382</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">31 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">118</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peanut flour, defatted (174267)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">52.2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">327</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">48 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">157</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soy flour, defatted (174275)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">51.5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">327</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">49 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">159</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Milk, dry, nonfat, instant (172196)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">35.1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">358</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">71 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">255</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hemp seed, hulled (170148)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">31.6 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">553</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">79 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">438</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pumpkin seed kernels, dried (170556)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30.2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">559</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">83 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">463</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Milk, dry, whole (170876)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26.3 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">496</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">95 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">471</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chickpea flour, besan (174288)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22.4 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">387</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">112 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">432</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Almonds (170567)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21.2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">579</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">118 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">683</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sunflower seed kernels (170562)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20.8 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">584</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">702</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oats, regular and quick, dry (173904)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">13.2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">379</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">189 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">718</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey, sweet, dried (171283)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12.9 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">353</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">194 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">684</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read the last two columns before the first. Hemp hearts and pumpkin seeds look respectable at around 30 percent protein, but each brings close to half its weight as fat, so 25 g of protein from hemp costs about 438 calories. Oats are the most common suggestion in homemade protein powder recipes online, yet you would need 189 g of dry oats, roughly two heaped cups, and about 718 calories to reach 25 g. That is breakfast for two, not a scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nonfat dry milk is the workhorse. Instant nonfat dry milk carries 35.1 g of protein and only 0.72 g of fat per 100 g, and the protein is milk protein, roughly four parts casein to one part whey, which is the same raw material whey powders are made from. The catch is the other half of the powder: USDA lists 52.2 g of carbohydrate per 100 g, nearly all of it lactose. If you are lactose intolerant, a milk-based homemade powder is the wrong tool, and a tub from our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder</a> list will sit better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Powdered peanut butter is the closest thing to a ready-made plant protein flour. USDA lists defatted peanut flour at 52.2 percent protein, and Original PB2 states 6 g of protein per 13 g serving, which works out near 46 percent once its added sugar and salt are counted. Our <a href="/pb2-protein-powder-review/">PB2 protein powder review</a> covers why only PB2 Performance is built as a true protein powder. Dried egg white is the outlier: at 81.1 percent, it is already an industrially concentrated protein, so adding it to a homemade blend is really buying protein powder under a grocery label.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE MYTH</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">You cannot make whey protein at home</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Straining yogurt or cheese leaves liquid whey that is mostly water and lactose. Even fully dried, sweet whey is only 12.9 percent protein per USDA. The 80 to 90 percent whey in a tub comes from industrial membrane filtration, not a kitchen.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="recipes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Do You Make Homemade Protein Powder? Four Recipes With the Math
          </h2>

          <p>
            To make homemade protein powder, weigh the dry ingredients on a kitchen scale, pulse any seeds or nuts briefly in a blender or spice grinder, whisk everything together, and store it in an airtight jar. Weigh rather than measure by cups: a cup of dry milk and a cup of hemp hearts differ widely in weight, and every number below assumes grams. The protein and calorie figures come from the USDA values in the table above and are our arithmetic, not lab tests of a finished batch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recipe 1: Chocolate Peanut Milk Powder (38% protein)</h3>

          <p>
            Mix 300 g instant nonfat dry milk, 100 g defatted peanut flour or powdered peanut butter, and 30 g unsweetened cocoa powder. The 430 g batch holds about 163 g of protein and 1,469 calories, so a 66 g serving, roughly two-thirds of a cup, gives about 25 g of protein and 225 calories. It is the best all-round homemade protein powder recipe for taste and cost, and it dissolves in milk or water better than seed blends. Expect about 32 g of carbohydrate per serving, mostly lactose from the milk powder. If you use Original PB2 instead of plain peanut flour, the protein dips slightly, because PB2 runs near 46 rather than 52 percent protein.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Recipe 1 ingredient</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Grams</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Instant nonfat dry milk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">300 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">105.3 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,074</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Defatted peanut flour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">52.2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">327</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Unsweetened cocoa</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.9 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">68</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Batch total</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">430 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">163.4 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,469</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Per 66 g serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">66 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 225</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recipe 2: Vegan Hemp and Pumpkin Seed Blend (37% protein)</h3>

          <p>
            Combine 150 g hulled hemp seeds, 100 g pumpkin seed kernels and 100 g defatted peanut flour. Pulse the seeds with the peanut flour in short bursts; the dry flour absorbs the seed oil and stops the mix turning into seed butter. The 350 g batch holds about 130 g of protein and 1,716 calories, so 67 g gives 25 g of protein at about 330 calories. That is roughly 100 more calories than recipe 1 for the same protein, almost all of it from healthy but dense seed fats. Peanut adds lysine, the amino acid seeds run lowest in, which is why a legume plus seed pairing beats seeds alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recipe 3: Egg White Boost Blend (55% protein)</h3>

          <p>
            Mix 200 g pasteurized dried egg white powder, 200 g instant nonfat dry milk and 30 g cocoa. The batch carries about 238 g of protein and 1,548 calories, so a 45 g serving delivers about 25 g of protein for roughly 162 calories, the leanest result any homemade recipe here reaches. Be clear about why: two-thirds of the protein comes from the egg white, which is a factory-dried product. If you are paying for that, compare it with the finished tubs in our <a href="/best-egg-white-protein-powder/">best egg white protein powder</a> guide, which add flavor and mixability.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recipe 4: Oat, Almond and Milk Breakfast Blend (21% protein)</h3>

          <p>
            Blend 200 g rolled oats to a flour, then add 100 g ground almonds and 100 g nonfat dry milk. The 400 g batch has about 83 g of protein and 1,695 calories. A 60 g serving gives around 12 g of protein and 254 calories; reaching 25 g takes 121 g and about 512 calories. This is the version many diy protein powder posts share, and it is a good overnight-oats or smoothie base. It is not a protein powder in any useful sense, and calling it one is how people end up drinking 500-calorie shakes while thinking they are on a light snack.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-not-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Can't Homemade Protein Powder Reach 70-90% Protein Like Whey Isolate?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Homemade protein powder cannot reach 70 to 90 percent protein because every whole food carries fat, carbohydrate, fiber or lactose alongside its protein, and removing them takes industrial separation that a blender cannot do. Grinding concentrates nothing; it only changes particle size. The protein percentage of a homemade blend is simply the weighted average of its ingredients, so it can never beat the richest thing you put in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Commercial powders get past that ceiling with processing steps. Whey isolate starts as the liquid left after cheesemaking, which is then filtered through membranes that hold back protein while water, lactose and minerals pass through, and finally spray-dried. Whey protein isolate is generally defined as at least 90 percent protein on a dry basis, which is why a typical isolate scoop puts about 25 g of protein in roughly 28 to 30 g of powder, and a little over 100 calories. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> page explains the filtration difference in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant protein powders use a similar trick. Pea protein is wet-extracted: the starch and fiber are separated from the protein in water and the protein fraction is dried, which is how pea isolates reach the 80 percent range. Hemp protein powder is made from the pressed cake left after hemp oil is extracted, which is why a store-bought hemp powder runs far higher than the 31.6 percent of whole hulled hemp seeds. You can press oil at home in theory, but not cleanly or safely enough to matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein quality matters too. Milk and egg proteins are complete, highly digestible and rich in leucine, the amino acid most tied to triggering muscle protein synthesis. Seed and grain proteins tend to be lower in lysine and somewhat less digestible, so a homemade vegan blend gives a little less usable protein per gram than its label math suggests. Blending a legume such as peanut or chickpea with seeds closes much of that gap, and for most people eating varied meals across a day, it is a minor issue rather than a deal-breaker.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes homemade protein powder useless. It means you should judge it as a protein-enriched food rather than a supplement. If the goal is hitting the daily target from our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide while eating in a calorie deficit, the denser commercial powders win. If the goal is adding 15 to 25 g of protein to a smoothie for an active teenager or someone trying to gain weight, the extra calories in a homemade blend are a feature.
          </p>
        </div>

