          <p>
            Pumpkin seeds, almonds and oats did not have prices we could pin to a consistent, comparable listing, so judge them by grams instead: 83 g of pumpkin seeds, 118 g of almonds or 189 g of oats for each 25 g of protein. Priced per pound, nuts and seeds are rarely cheap enough to compete once you need that much of them. That is why recipe 1, which leans on dry milk, is the only homemade blend that saves meaningful money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store-bought powder also has advantages a price table misses: flavoring, mixability, a measured scoop, and for some brands a third-party certification for contaminants and banned substances. If cost is the main reason you are searching for a homemade recipe, our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> rankings compare finished tubs on the same cost per 25 g basis, and our <a href="/sprouts-protein-powder/">Sprouts protein powder</a> guide covers a store where bulk-bin seeds and finished powders sit in the same aisle.
