        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Homemade Protein Powder Safe? Raw Egg, Raw Flour and Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Homemade protein powder is safe when every ingredient is already ready to eat: pasteurized dried egg white rather than raw or home-dried eggs, toasted rather than raw chickpea or grain flour, and dry ingredients stored airtight. The three real risks are Salmonella from raw egg, E. coli and Salmonella from raw flour, and rancid fats from ground seeds and nuts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never add raw egg or raw egg white to a shake. The <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/what-you-need-know-about-egg-safety" rel="noopener">FDA's egg-safety guidance</a> warns that raw eggs can carry Salmonella even when the shell looks clean, and it singles out older adults, young children, pregnant women and people with weakened immune systems as most at risk. Dried egg white sold in the US as an egg product is pasteurized under federal egg-products rules, which is why it is the only safe way to add egg protein to an uncooked drink. Dehydrating your own eggs is widely discouraged by food-safety programs, because a home dehydrator cannot be relied on to reach temperatures that kill Salmonella.
          </p>

          <p>
            Raw flour is the less obvious risk. The <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/handling-flour-safely-what-you-need-know" rel="noopener">FDA's flour guidance</a> says plainly never to eat or taste raw flour, dough or batter, and it notes several outbreaks of foodborne illness tied to raw flour since 2009. Flour is a raw agricultural product that is not treated to kill pathogens. If you want chickpea or oat flour in a no-cook shake, buy a product labeled heat-treated or ready to eat, or toast it in a dry pan until fragrant and let it cool before mixing. Rolled oats are steamed during processing, but blending them does not add any further kill step.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage depends on fat. A milk-and-peanut-flour blend has very little fat and keeps well in an airtight jar in a cool, dark cupboard, much like the tubs in our <a href="/does-protein-powder-expire/">does protein powder expire</a> guide. Blends with ground hemp, pumpkin seeds or almonds go rancid much faster, because grinding exposes their oils to air. Make those in small batches, keep them in the fridge or freezer, and throw out any batch that smells like paint or old crayons.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the earliest best-by date among the ingredients as the batch's own date, keep a dry scoop in the jar, and never mix a shake ahead and leave it out. Once liquid is added, a homemade shake is perishable food: refrigerate it and drink it the same day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="allergens" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Allergens and Health Issues Should You Watch With DIY Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every high-protein homemade ingredient is a major allergen: milk, egg, peanut, tree nuts such as almonds, and soy are all among the nine major food allergens identified by US law, per the <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/food-labeling-nutrition/food-allergies" rel="noopener">FDA</a>. Hemp and pumpkin seeds are not on that list, but they are often packed in facilities that also handle nuts and sesame, so check the bag's advisory statement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homemade powders also lack the allergen labeling a commercial tub carries, which matters the moment you share a batch or bring shakes to a team practice. Label the jar with every ingredient. If anyone in the household has a peanut or tree nut allergy, use a dedicated blender jar or skip those ingredients entirely, because ground nut residue is hard to clean out of blades and gaskets.
          </p>

          <p>
            The calorie gap is the most common health surprise. A homemade seed blend can add 300 to 450 calories to a smoothie before fruit and milk are counted, which can quietly stall a weight-loss plan. Track the grams, not the scoops. For a plant-based option with fewer calories per gram of protein, the store-bought tubs in our <a href="/best-hemp-protein-powder/">best hemp protein powder</a> guide and the hemp powder covered in our <a href="/bobs-red-mill-protein-powder-review/">Bob's Red Mill protein powder review</a> start from pressed hemp cake rather than whole seeds.
          </p>

          <p>
            High protein intake is not right for everyone. People with chronic kidney disease are often advised to limit protein, and anyone who is pregnant, managing a medical condition, taking medication or caring for an older adult with health problems should ask a doctor or registered dietitian before adding regular protein shakes. Dry milk blends also bring lactose, and powdered peanut butter can bring added sugar and salt depending on the brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about what you actually need. If your problem is joint or skin support rather than total protein, collagen is a different product with a different amino acid profile, covered in our <a href="/collagen-vs-protein-powder/">collagen vs protein powder</a> comparison. If your problem is simply getting enough protein, food first and a well-chosen powder second remains the simplest plan, and a homemade blend is a reasonable middle path for people who want whole-food ingredients.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using oats as the protein base</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dry oats are only 13.2 percent protein per USDA, so 25 g of protein takes 189 g of oats and about 718 calories. Oats belong in a homemade blend for texture and carbohydrate, not as the protein source. Build the base on dry milk, peanut flour or egg white instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding raw egg to shakes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Raw eggs can carry Salmonella even when the shell looks clean, according to FDA egg-safety guidance. Use pasteurized dried egg white or liquid pasteurized egg whites from a carton, and never dry your own eggs in a home dehydrator, which cannot reliably reach a safe temperature.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring by cups instead of grams</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cup of hemp hearts and a cup of dry milk weigh very different amounts, so cup-based recipes can be off by a third or more. Weigh every ingredient on a kitchen scale and weigh each serving too, or your protein and calorie math means nothing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Making big batches of seed blends</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ground hemp, pumpkin seeds and almonds expose their oils to air and turn rancid far faster than whole seeds. Make seed-based homemade protein powder in batches you will finish within a few weeks, store it cold, and discard anything that smells sharp or paint-like.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming homemade is always cheaper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Only nonfat dry milk clearly undercuts store-bought powder, at about $0.67 per 25 g of protein at the Amazon price we saw. Hemp hearts and PB2 cost about as much per gram of protein as a budget whey, and small bags of seeds cost more.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How do you make homemade protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Weigh high-protein dry ingredients such as nonfat dry milk, defatted peanut flour, hemp hearts or pasteurized egg white powder, pulse any seeds briefly in a blender, whisk everything together and store it in an airtight jar. A simple mix of 300 g dry milk, 100 g peanut flour and 30 g cocoa gives about 25 g of protein per 66 g serving, per USDA values.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the highest-protein ingredient for homemade protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Dried egg white is the highest at 81.1 g of protein per 100 g in USDA FoodData Central, followed by defatted peanut flour at 52.2 g and defatted soy flour at 51.5 g. Nonfat dry milk carries about 35 g. Whole seeds such as hemp and pumpkin sit near 30 g but bring about half their weight as fat.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you make whey protein powder at home?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Liquid whey from straining yogurt or making cheese is mostly water and lactose, and even fully dried sweet whey is only 12.9 percent protein per USDA. Commercial whey concentrate and isolate reach 80 to 90 percent through industrial membrane filtration and spray-drying. Nonfat dry milk is the closest kitchen substitute, with milk protein at about 35 percent.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is homemade protein powder as good as store-bought?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is less concentrated. A homemade blend usually lands between 20 and 55 percent protein, so you need 45 to 120 g of powder and more calories to get 25 g of protein, compared with about 30 g of a whey isolate. Milk and egg based blends offer complete protein, while seed blends benefit from adding a legume such as peanut.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is homemade protein powder cheaper?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Sometimes. Nonfat dry milk worked out to about $0.67 per 25 g of protein at the Amazon price we saw, well below premium whey at about $1.84. Hemp hearts at about $1.42 and PB2 at about $1.19 per 25 g are no cheaper than budget whey. Nuts and seeds bought in small packs usually cost more than finished powder.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How long does homemade protein powder last?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It depends on the fat content. A low-fat blend of dry milk, peanut flour and cocoa keeps like other dry pantry goods when sealed and stored cool, and should be used by the earliest best-by date of its ingredients. Blends with ground seeds or nuts go rancid much faster, so make small batches, refrigerate or freeze them and finish them within a few weeks.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I use protein powder from hemp seeds I grind myself?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  You can, but ground hulled hemp seeds are only 31.6 percent protein and about 49 percent fat per USDA, so 25 g of protein costs about 438 calories. Commercial hemp protein powder is made from the pressed cake left after oil extraction, which is why it is far more concentrated. Pulse hemp with a dry flour so it does not turn into seed butter.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

